1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,420
This is an AI agent being stopped in the middle of an edit.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:06,760
Not by a test, not by a merge conflict.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,520
By another agent's change, made a few seconds earlier,

4
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:11,920
in a different checkout.

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:13,700
This is Weft.

6
00:00:14,628 --> 00:00:18,328
Git and GitHub coordinate people at merge time.

7
00:00:18,708 --> 00:00:23,648
You branch, you work for a day, and the pull request tells you what collided.

8
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:28,408
That works when changes are slow and humans read every diff.

9
00:00:29,088 --> 00:00:30,088
Agents are not slow.

10
00:00:30,088 --> 00:00:33,968
Ten of them can touch the same function in the same minute,

11
00:00:34,428 --> 00:00:38,628
each in its own checkout, each sure its picture of the code is current.

12
00:00:39,268 --> 00:00:42,708
By the time a pull request exists, the damage is done:

13
00:00:42,708 --> 00:00:46,288
two branches that merge cleanly and do not compile.

14
00:00:47,028 --> 00:00:52,168
Agents need coordination at edit time, while the code is being written.

15
00:00:53,471 --> 00:00:57,631
Weft is a coordination layer for coding agents, built on Cloudflare.

16
00:00:58,251 --> 00:01:03,191
Every tool call that edits code becomes an event in one strictly ordered log per

17
00:01:03,191 --> 00:01:04,191
repository.

18
00:01:04,631 --> 00:01:10,211
A sequencer, one Durable Object per repo, checks each edit against everything that

19
00:01:10,211 --> 00:01:14,531
landed since the agent last looked, down to the symbol.

20
00:01:14,531 --> 00:01:19,471
Nothing is locked; agents keep working, and Weft validates instead.

21
00:01:19,471 --> 00:01:23,771
The verdict goes straight back into the agent's run as a diagnostic:

22
00:01:23,771 --> 00:01:26,471
the multi-agent version of your editor's red squiggle.

23
00:01:27,011 --> 00:01:31,271
The wire format is an open protocol, the Weft Coordination Protocol.

24
00:01:32,659 --> 00:01:35,059
Here is the moment it exists for.

25
00:01:35,519 --> 00:01:38,199
Two agents from two vendors.

26
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:43,799
Claude Code is adding session expiry, so it changes createSession to take an options

27
00:01:45,479 --> 00:01:46,479
argument.

28
00:01:46,479 --> 00:01:50,339
Codex, in its own checkout, planned a signup flow against the old signature.

29
00:01:51,239 --> 00:01:53,759
Codex tries to write its call.

30
00:01:53,759 --> 00:02:00,899
The pre-edit hook asks Weft, Weft answers no, and the edit never touches disk.

31
00:02:00,899 --> 00:02:05,199
Codex gets the reason in its tool result: you use createSession,

32
00:02:05,539 --> 00:02:09,559
whose signature changed in event three, by Claude,

33
00:02:09,559 --> 00:02:11,079
after your base.

34
00:02:11,079 --> 00:02:17,879
Below it, the actual diff, because Claude's change is not in Codex's checkout yet.

35
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:20,459
Codex reads it, passes a one-hour time-to-live, and retries.

36
00:02:20,459 --> 00:02:21,459
Accepted.

37
00:02:21,699 --> 00:02:24,379
Both branches merge, and main type-checks.

38
00:02:24,379 --> 00:02:27,519
The conflict was resolved before either commit existed.

39
00:02:27,519 --> 00:02:32,939
Only one side was told to yield: the agent that was first keeps the function,

40
00:02:32,939 --> 00:02:36,259
so two agents never back off from each other forever.

41
00:02:37,059 --> 00:02:39,059
Each check costs about half a second.

42
00:02:40,374 --> 00:02:42,394
Being stopped is not the only option.

43
00:02:42,954 --> 00:02:47,594
The losing agent can retreat, wait, negotiate, or escalate,

44
00:02:48,334 --> 00:02:50,194
and each one is a protocol action.

45
00:02:50,734 --> 00:02:56,174
Here, the second agent proposes a deal: keep the old one-argument form as an

46
00:02:56,174 --> 00:02:57,174
overload.

47
00:02:57,254 --> 00:03:00,914
The proposal is injected into the first agent's next tool call.

48
00:03:01,554 --> 00:03:08,254
It accepts, writes real TypeScript overloads, and Weft won't let it finish until it

49
00:03:08,254 --> 00:03:09,254
has.

50
00:03:09,254 --> 00:03:10,334
Both changes land.

51
00:03:10,894 --> 00:03:12,294
Five out of five runs.

52
00:03:13,682 --> 00:03:15,822
Above the log sits a thin platform.

53
00:03:16,522 --> 00:03:21,562
A task can get several candidate changes, each in its own fork in Cloudflare

54
00:03:21,562 --> 00:03:22,562
Artifacts.

55
00:03:22,802 --> 00:03:26,342
Every push is rebased onto trunk, tested in a sandbox,

56
00:03:27,002 --> 00:03:31,342
previewed straight from the Artifacts commit, screenshotted with Browser Rendering

57
00:03:31,342 --> 00:03:36,042
and pixel-diffed against trunk, then scored by a review agent against the task's

58
00:03:36,042 --> 00:03:37,042
acceptance criteria.

59
00:03:37,042 --> 00:03:42,122
Evidence binds to a stable Change-Id, not a commit hash,

60
00:03:42,122 --> 00:03:44,142
so it survives every rebase.

61
00:03:44,142 --> 00:03:50,642
For "add kiwi to the pricing page", one candidate changed the pricing page and

62
00:03:50,642 --> 00:03:51,642
scored a hundred.

63
00:03:51,642 --> 00:03:56,002
The other also rewrote the home page hero and failed review.

64
00:03:56,762 --> 00:04:00,842
Weft landed the right one on its own, about eighty seconds after the push.

65
00:04:01,642 --> 00:04:03,782
Risky work waits for a human to approve.

66
00:04:05,093 --> 00:04:06,313
Landing is not the end.

67
00:04:06,573 --> 00:04:10,633
We planted a bug, a missing null check that unit tests don't cover,

68
00:04:10,893 --> 00:04:11,893
and let it land.

69
00:04:12,693 --> 00:04:14,873
Workers Builds deployed it from the Artifacts trunk.

70
00:04:15,553 --> 00:04:17,733
Six seconds later, production was throwing.

71
00:04:18,273 --> 00:04:20,993
A Tail Worker fed every exception into a detector;

72
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:26,133
about fifteen seconds after the land, it started a revert from the operation log.

73
00:04:26,913 --> 00:04:30,153
Forty-three seconds after the land, the fix was live again,

74
00:04:30,493 --> 00:04:34,693
the task reopened with the stack trace attached, and the agent was told why.

75
00:04:35,213 --> 00:04:36,313
No human touched it.

76
00:04:37,441 --> 00:04:39,861
Weft does not care which agent you use.

77
00:04:40,501 --> 00:04:43,301
Each harness gets a thin adapter over its own hooks,

78
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:45,641
and they all speak the same protocol.

79
00:04:46,521 --> 00:04:50,601
Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode run live against one coordinator,

80
00:04:50,601 --> 00:04:55,421
and a plain file watcher covers any tool with no hooks at all.

81
00:04:56,201 --> 00:05:00,321
Hermes, the agent that built this project, has its own adapter too.

82
00:05:00,321 --> 00:05:05,241
Adapters declare what they can do: observe, inject context,

83
00:05:05,981 --> 00:05:09,401
block an edit, or refuse to finish while an error is open.

84
00:05:10,677 --> 00:05:14,237
When agents write the code, nobody is watching an editor.

85
00:05:14,937 --> 00:05:16,877
So Weft's human surface is a feed.

86
00:05:17,537 --> 00:05:22,417
This is Hermes, on a phone: every intent, edit, squiggle,

87
00:05:22,917 --> 00:05:27,297
negotiation, landing and revert, live, across repos.

88
00:05:28,017 --> 00:05:30,117
Tap a squiggle to see what caused it.

89
00:05:30,597 --> 00:05:34,277
Approve a candidate, undo a landing, pause an agent,

90
00:05:34,597 --> 00:05:36,857
or send it a message, from wherever you are.

91
00:05:36,857 --> 00:05:39,197
The same feed runs in a browser.

92
00:05:40,625 --> 00:05:43,205
Weft was built by agents, under Weft.

93
00:05:43,765 --> 00:05:48,785
Since the first night of the build, every kanban worker on this project ran the Weft

94
00:05:48,785 --> 00:05:49,785
adapter.

95
00:05:49,885 --> 00:05:57,805
In ten hours, its own log recorded 571 events: 420 edits from three agent profiles

96
00:05:57,805 --> 00:06:05,325
across 22 tasks, 66 checkpoints and 18 landings, with 59 warnings that told one

97
00:06:05,325 --> 00:06:06,665
agent to wait for another.

98
00:06:07,245 --> 00:06:10,405
The first time two workers reached for the same function,

99
00:06:10,765 --> 00:06:14,525
the second was told to wait, waited, and finished cleanly.

100
00:06:15,065 --> 00:06:16,065
This is that log.

101
00:06:17,341 --> 00:06:19,081
The Agent Plugins 1.

102
00:06:19,081 --> 00:06:23,901
0 spec says it plainly: hooks don't yet have consistent semantics across clients,

103
00:06:23,901 --> 00:06:26,081
so they are not portable.

104
00:06:26,081 --> 00:06:28,701
Coordination needs exactly that.

105
00:06:28,701 --> 00:06:34,681
WCP is our answer: a small, versioned wire protocol with a conformance suite and a

106
00:06:35,341 --> 00:06:36,341
reference implementation.

107
00:06:36,341 --> 00:06:40,381
It's MIT-licensed, and we're proposing it as an open standard to the Agentic AI

108
00:06:40,381 --> 00:06:41,381
Foundation.

109
00:06:42,225 --> 00:06:45,965
Everything runs on Cloudflare, and every product carries weight.

110
00:06:46,665 --> 00:06:49,805
Artifacts holds every repo, fork and preview.

111
00:06:50,685 --> 00:06:52,325
Durable Objects are the sequencers.

112
00:06:53,105 --> 00:06:57,205
Workflows run rebase, landing, revert and best-of-N.

113
00:06:57,205 --> 00:07:01,145
Containers run agents and git with no secrets inside.

114
00:07:01,145 --> 00:07:07,085
Queues carry pushes and production errors, Tail Workers watch production,

115
00:07:07,965 --> 00:07:13,705
Browser Rendering takes the screenshots, Workers AI and AI Gateway run the reviewer,

116
00:07:14,385 --> 00:07:16,545
D1 and R2 hold the evidence.

117
00:07:17,305 --> 00:07:18,305
Weft.

118
00:07:18,305 --> 00:07:19,685
Coordination at edit time.
